From: levy@cs.utk.edu
Date: Fri, 28 Oct 1994 16:27:49 -0400 (EDT)
Subject: [#94-10-586] Anti-Statism Not Consistent with Boundless Expansion

> 
> From: "Kobielus, James" <KobielJ@usva8.dyncorp.com>
> 
> Boundless expansion--throughout outer and inner space--seems to call for a 
> strong, enduring, well-heeled megastate, megacorporation, and/or 
> megareligion supported by all humanity.
> 

Jim,

You've lived so long under statist control that you have fallen into the trap
of believing that the government actually provides anything.  By defintion, 
governments only provide regulation, backed by coercive force.  Any *services* 
(police, schools, roads) that exist exist because a sufficient number of 
people want them and willingly pay for them.  An anti-statist position claims 
that such services would work better if paid for (and received) by *only* those 
people who want them.  The size of the provider makes no difference to the 
argument -- if it ends up that a huge "megacorporation" can provide 
intergalactic service more efficiently than lots of small corporations, I don't 
see why this should bother anyone -- unless the corporation (or anyone else) 
*forces* them to pay for unwanted services.

Arguments against this viewpoint, like the ones I heard over and over in the
(otherwise good) public schools I attended, tend to break down in light of
actual examples of what happened when now-public services came from the 
private sector.  D. Friedman's _The Machinery of Freedom_ contains many such 
examples. A more recent example: AT&T no longer has monopolistic control over 
long-distance telephone services, which ends up meaning that I can now make 
certain long-distance phone calls (using, e.g., MCI Friends & Family) for 
practically nothing.  When I hear claims about how terrible certain services 
would become if run commercially ("Privatizing the mail service means Partial 
Post", according to one bumper sticker), I recall similar fears expressed 
during the planning phases of the AT&T breakup.  The superior on-time delivery 
records of private carriers like Federal Express, over the miserable rates of 
the US Post office, provide another example.  I stopped shipping my urgent 
correpsondence by "ExpressMail" after two successive late deliveries (~1 week) 
of next-day packages.  Since then I've used FedEx without any such problems.

Does this mean that private companies do things perfectly?  No.  It just means
that they do them better than government.  People who still favor government
solutions will often argue against such evidence by comparing apples with 
oranges -- what governments *would* do under ideal circumstance (no 
corruption, no influence peddling) with what the free-market *actually*
does under *real* circumstances (which often include artifically-induced
adversity from government regulation).

		*		*		*

I remember reading, in James Frazer's _The Golden Bough_, a description
of the origins of kingship.  Contrary to common-sense -- e.g., that
the "the biggest guy with the biggest club" made himself king, as my 
eigth-grade social-studies teacher told us -- kingship perpetuated itself 
under the subjects' belief that the king's well being meant the people's well
being -- i.e., the king served very much as a fetish-object, like
the voodoo dolls still found in some places today.  Harming the king
would harm the people; sustaining him would sustain them.  I suspect
that much of the appeal of government comes from a similar gut-level
superstition -- the fear that we cannot take care of ourselves and must
therefore invest our well-being in some other person or set of persons 
whose continued existence, despite all evidence to the contrary, 
we require in order to survive and prosper.  

If you think that such primitive beliefs have long since passed away,
look at film clips of Hitler's appearances at public rallies -- the
crowds of young people literally swooning in his presence.  Or think
of how millions of American reacted to the assassination of JFK -- the
sense that somehow the demise of Camelot (!) meant doom for us all.

In fact, it surprises me how much emphasis historians give to the
shift from divine-right kingship to constitutional democrcay.  Anthropo-
logically and psychologically, the origins of both strike me as 
depressingly similar.

--
Simon D. Levy			levy@cs.utk.edu

