From: Anders Sandberg <nv91-asa@nada.kth.se>
Date: Sat, 29 Oct 1994 00:04:06 +0100 (MET)
Subject: [#94-10-592] BABY: Reproduction

Rob Mutschler wrote:

>     It seems like there will be a few generations of children 
>that will not be able to alter their "picked" genes.  This to
>me, seems that there will be a high tendancy for parent to pick
>genes that are common and possible accepted as the normal, or
>popular characteristics of their cultures.  This would not only
>make for a bland view of the people that are around (high 
>percentages of tall people, people that look like stars of pop 
>culture from 20 years ago, ect.),

This is a bit self-limiting. Humans, like most animals, seem to
prefer individuals who on one hand conform to some general
standards, but on the other hand are different in some ways from
the mean population. This is clearly seen in the fashion industry,
where the designer who dares to create a sufficiently different
style has a good chance of starting a new fashion. So in the case
of "fashion-children", the first generation might be almost
completely tall, blond and blue-eyed :-). But then many parents
will want to make their children a bit different, so new fashions
will appear. Its an open question whether single fashions would
form ("Peter must be around 15, since he is so black and short.
Born in those PC 2010's, you know..."), or if the diversity would
increase (might be a good cure for racism?). 


> but could also cause a 
>detrimental gap in the gene pool. (?) I don't know enogh of
>of the genetic diversity that is needed to survive possible 
>rogue viruses and other such plagues.

I seriously doubt that genetic design would decrease the diversity
that much. Since (at least at first) most changes would be removal
of genetic disorders and cosmetic changes, only a very small subset
of the genome would change. Unless there appear diseases or viruses
which attack certain popular genes (like some instability in a very
popular dark skin tone causing malign melanoma), there should not
be that big risks. 

And we could always try to increase the genetic diversity a bit... 


